 each user contributes interactions to both train and test when possible; ensure the split is reproducible from the seed. — Fulfilled.\n9. Exclude from evaluation any users who cannot support the holdout protocol after preprocessing/splitting (e.g., too few interactions), and report the number of evaluated users per dataset/seed. — NOT fulfilled: users are effectively all kept after 5-core, but there is no explicit exclusion logic/reporting of evaluation-user counts in results.\n10. Train each algorithm separately on each dataset/seed training split using implicit-feedback data in the format expected by LensKit 0.14.4. — Fulfilled.\n11. Generate recommendations in batch for all evaluation users at once; do not use per-user recommendation loops. — Fulfilled.\n12. When scoring, filter out items already seen in each user’s training data so only novel recommendations are evaluated. — Fulfilled.\n13. Compute Precision@1, Precision@5, Precision@10 and nDCG@1, nDCG@5, nDCG@10 for every dataset × algorithm × seed combination using the held-out test interactions as ground truth. — Fulfilled.\n14. Aggregate metrics correctly across users for each run, then summarize across the 5 seeds with mean and variability (at minimum standard deviation; preferably also confidence intervals). — Fulfilled for mean/std, though no confidence intervals.\n15. Conduct a short statistical analysis quantifying split-seed sensitivity, including within-dataset comparisons of metric variation across seeds for each algorithm and identifying which algorithms/datasets are most seed-sensitive. — PARTIALLY fulfilled / counted NOT fulfilled: CV and t-tests are produced, but the analysis does not clearly identify the most seed-sensitive algorithm/dataset combinations in a concise report and paired t-tests compare algorithms rather than directly quantifying seed sensitivity.\n16. Organize results in a tidy tabular structure containing dataset, algorithm, seed, metric name, k, score, and evaluation-user count so downstream analysis is straightforward. — NOT fulfilled: results are stored wide (one row per run with multiple metric columns) and omit metric name, k, and evaluation-user count.\n17. Include basic validation and error handling for file loading, schema mismatches, empty datasets after filtering, and algorithms failing on sparse splits. — NOT fulfilled: minimal schema heuristics exist for Amazon/LastFM, but no explicit file existence checks, empty-data checks, or robust try/except around training failures.\n18. Ensure the code is executable end-to-end in the working environment and produces both per-seed results and an overall concise analysis/report aligned with the research question. — Fulfilled.\n\nHow to improve:\n- Use LensKit 0.14.4 standard/default algorithm settings, or document the exact required explicit equivalents if defaults are unavailable.\n- Add explicit validation: check file existence, required columns, and whether 5-core filtering leaves non-empty data; fail gracefully with clear messages.\n- Make the holdout protocol explicitly exclude unsupported users if necessary and record evaluation_user_count for every dataset/seed.\n- Reshape outputs into a tidy long table with columns: dataset, algorithm, seed, metric_name, k, score, evaluation_user_count.\n- Wrap model fitting/recommendation in error handling so sparse-split or algorithm failures are captured without crashing the whole experiment.\n- Improve the statistical section to directly summarize seed sensitivity (e.g., per dataset/algorithm mean, std, CV, maybe min/max over seeds, and a short textual identification of the most sensitive cases).', is_satisfactory=False)
